{
  "abstract": "We prove that every private five- or six-clique whose vertices have total degree at most seven admits an ambient seven-color reduction in any graph of outerthickness at most two. The replacement deletes the clique and adds at most two boundary stars, preserving every exterior vertex and edge; every seven-coloring of the replacement extends. The six-clique proof combines a two-cover lemma for ordinary graphs with a private-component classification and outerplanar edge counts. The five-clique proof explicitly imports published triangle-shadow coloring lemmas and supplies new outerplanar linking and exhaustive small residual geometry. We also prove a four-clique reduction when its outside-neighbor union has at most five vertices or its four outside-neighbor sets share three actual vertices. Its boundary can have seven vertices, and the replacement may identify a nonadjacent pair. Consequently the degree-seven graph of a minimum-order non-seven-colorable outerthickness-two graph is a Gallai forest with clique blocks of order at most four. For $\\ell$ low vertices in $c$ components it has at most $2(\\ell-c)$ internal edges and at least $3\\ell+4c$ edges to the remainder. Arbitrarily many mixed private modules of these types over a host of order at most 28 are seven-colorable whenever the full graph is outerthickness two. Compact standard-library programs regenerate every new finite geometric premise; no discovery solver verdict is assumed. The reductions do not establish that their configurations must occur, and the unrestricted chromatic and Earth–Moon problems remain open.",
